  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 106 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nThis Morning and Early Afternoon\n\nA shortwave trough over southwest Minnesota will move east\novernight and this morning. As this system approaches, the 850 \nmb moisture transport will gradually increase on the nose of a \n30-40 knot nocturnal jet. Showers and maybe a few storms will \nmove into northeast Iowa and southeast Minnesota after 3 am, and\nwestern Wisconsin around 6 am. These showers and storms will \nmove out of northeast Iowa and southeast Minnesota around noon \nand western Wisconsin by 3 pm. \n\nThe 29.00z HREF has a mean 24-hour rainfall of a 0.25 to 0.50\ninches from northeast Iowa northeast into central and north-\ncentral Wisconsin, and up to a quarter-inch in southwest\nWisconsin. The ensemble localized probability-matched mean\nsuggests that there could be some localized rain totals of a \nhalf to 1-inch.\n\nIn the wake of this system, some of the CAMs are continuing to \nshow that subsidence in the wake of this system might bring \nstronger winds (up to 45 mph) between 925 and 850 mb to the \nsurface mainly west of the Mississippi River. The evening shift \nused the RAP to raise the Issued at 106 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026wind\ngusts into the 30 to 40 mph range.\n\nThere is a trailing shortwave trough over central South Dakota.\nThere is ample instability ahead of this wave at the apex of a\nstrong ridge. This has resulted in some strong to severe storms.\nThe CAMS are in general agreement that this shortwave will move\nsoutheast of the area late this morning and afternoon. Thoughts\nare that these storms will gradually become more elevated as the\nnight persists and this would likely continue through much of\nthis morning.\n\nRain-cooled air will slow the warming this afternoon. High\ntemperatures will range from the upper 60s in north-central\nWisconsin to around 80 west of the Mississippi River.\n\nTonight and Sunday morning\n\nThe 500 mb longwave ridge axis will move east into the Upper\nMississippi River Valley. The nose 30-40 knot 850 mb jet will\ndevelop during the evening ahead of another shortwave trough\nmoving east on the north side of the 500 mb ridge. It continues\nto look like the better chances of showers and storms will be \nalong and north of Interstate 90. As the the nocturnal jet \ngradually veers overnight, the showers and storms will move out \nof the area on Sunday morning.\n\nWhile the 0-6 km shear is greater than 40 knots north of\nInterstate 94 tonight, the most-unstable CAPES are less than 500\nJ/kg, so the chances of organized severe weather looks low.\n\nLow temperatures tonight will be around 60 north of Interstate\n94 and in the 60s across the remainder of the area.\n\nSunday afternoon and night\n\nOn Sunday afternoon and night, the ridge axis will build north\nacross the Upper Mississippi River Valley. A frontal boundary \nwill be initially near Interstate 90 in the morning and then \nmove gradually north during the day. As this occurs, the strong \nwesterlies will shift northward into northern Minnesota and \nnorthern Wisconsin. This should result in lesser 0-3 km and 0-6 \nkm shear across the Upper Mississippi River Valley. Thus, \nthinking the marginal risk of severe weather should be shifted \nfurther north.\n\nHigh temperatures on Sunday will range from the upper 70s to\nmid-80s. Low temperatures on Sunday night will range from the\nmid-60s to lower 70s.\n\nMonday through Friday\n\nA northern stream shortwave trough will gradually flatten the\nridge on Monday night and Tuesday. This will leave the Upper\nMississippi River Valley on the northern periphery of this ridge\nthrough mid-week and then there are some indications that this\nridge may build northward across the region for late in the\nweek. Periodic showers and storms look possible during the mid-\nweek.\n\nTemperatures will be likely above normal through this time\nperiod. High temperatures will be mainly in the 80s. There may\nbe even a few lower 90s. Low temperatures will be in the 60s and\nlower 70s. There will be a possibility of near record warm lows\nacross Iowa and Illinois early in the week and again during the\nlate week.",
